{:edb/db-before-t 5765 :edb/db-after-t 5766 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539078 50 #inst "2026-10-23T17:57:36.896Z" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186581747 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}5{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186581747 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{4}5{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186582344 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186582344 1071 "Calculate $\\int x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186582344 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ where $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{1}{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186582344 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ where $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{1}{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186582346 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186582346 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186582348 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{17}(\n$$\n{{field-1}} $)$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186582348 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{17}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186582348 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4({e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-16I \\\\ 17I & =-{e}^{-4x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )))-(-\\frac{1}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}(-1+0)+\\frac{1}{17}(1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}+\\frac{1}{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}(1+{e}^{-4\\pi }).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186582348 1074 "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $I=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-4x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-4{e}^{-4x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4({e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-16I \\\\ 17I & =-{e}^{-4x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )))-(-\\frac{1}{17}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}(-1+0)+\\frac{1}{17}(1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4\\pi }}{17}+\\frac{1}{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}(1+{e}^{-4\\pi }).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186582360 1074 "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1).$ Then, since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(xy+yz+1)}^{3}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1)$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).$    \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot y)i+(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot (x+z))j+(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }i+\\frac{x+z}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1+1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $$\n\\displaystyle n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.\n$$ \n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a+b}{c}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=3.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186582360 1074 "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1).$ Then, since $\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(xy+yz+1)}^{3}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1)$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).$    \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy+yz+1))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot y)i+(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot (x+z))j+(\\frac{1}{xy+yz+1}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }i+\\frac{x+z}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{xy+yz+1}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1+1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.$ \n\nFinally, $\\fr