{7}\\right)}=4\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186585031 1074 "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/14/143519dfc291a32f5c617b70a4297643429b18eea64b7ad4736758c60c3a7e27.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{7}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{48}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}=4\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186585036 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{10x}$ on the interval $-\\infty <x<0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{10x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{10x}\\,\\text{d}x &={[\\frac{k}{10}{e}^{10x}]}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result b