consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 1,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{1}-0 \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186585090 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following properties:\n\n1\\. $$\n\\displaystyle {⋃}_{i=0}^{194}\\,[\\,i\\,]_{195}=Z\n$$\n\n2\\. $[\\,5\\,]_{195}\\cap [\\,127\\,]_{195}=∅$\n\n3\\. ${Z}_{195}=\\{[\\,i\\,]_{195}\\,∣\\,0\\le i<195\\}$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186585090 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following properties:\n\n1\\. ${⋃}_{i=0}^{194}\\,[\\,i\\,]_{195}=Z$\n\n2\\. $[\\,5\\,]_{195}\\cap [\\,127\\,]_{195}=∅$\n\n3\\. ${Z}_{195}=\\{[\\,i\\,]_{195}\\,∣\\,0\\le i<195\\}$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186585093 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{13\\pi }{4}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{13\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/e1/e169b457c3216ffb18463b3e37a345b708e2d5efb2af1076b25f397867d7a826.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+7=7-\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186585093 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{13\\pi }{4}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{13\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/e1/e169b457c3216ffb18463b3e37a345b708e2d5efb2af1076b25f397867d7a826.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+7=7-\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186585109 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{(4-x)^{3}}$ on the interval $x\\le 2,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}\\frac{k}{(4-x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}\\frac{k}{(4-x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x &=\\frac{k}{2(4-x)^{2}}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{2}=\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{8}=1\\,⟹\\,k=8.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186585109 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{(4-x)^{3}}$ on the interval $x\\le 2,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}\\frac{k}{(4-x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}\\frac{k}{(4-x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x &=\\frac{k}{2(4-x)^{2}}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{2}=\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{8}=1\\,⟹\\,k=8.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186585714 1071 "Given that $A=2$ and $B=4,$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186585714 1071 "Given that $A=2$ and $B=4,$\n\n$\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186585749 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=5\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186585749 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=5\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186585749 1074 "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186585749 1074 "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186585772 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186585772 1071 "$\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186585793 1071 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes 