4 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{360}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$-{360}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={0}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{360}^{∘})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({0}^{∘}).$\n\nThe angle ${0}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${0}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{∘}=1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{∘}}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{360}^{∘})=0.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186585794 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{360}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$-{360}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={0}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{360}^{∘})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({0}^{∘}).$\n\nThe angle ${0}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${0}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{∘}=1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatornam